  comparisonBefore: "TypeScript uses a regular expression for the same decimal spelling and Number.isSafeInteger for the range. number | undefined means a number or an absent value; undefined represents invalid input here.",
  comparisonAfter: "The six displayed lines are identical. parseInt can read the prefix of 12px, so it is not a substitute for this validation. Seseragi parse supplies the checks and named failure reasons; this comparison does not claim identical detailed diagnostics.",
  details: [
    "Accepted text is an optional + or -, followed by 0 or an ASCII decimal digit from 1 to 9 and further decimal digits. Empty text, surrounding whitespace, 012, 12.0, 1_000 and 0x10 are rejected. If trimming is desired, choose that separate step before parsing.",
    "Int ranges from -9007199254740991 through 9007199254740991. It cannot contain a fraction, NaN, infinity or negative zero. Negative integers are valid; a requirement such as a positive count needs a separate application check.",
    "Empty text gives EmptyInt; an invalid character gives InvalidIntDigit; an out-of-range value gives IntOutsideRange. InvalidIntDigit carries offset, a UTF-8 byte position, and radix, the numeric base, fixed at 10 here. The shared IntParseError also includes InvalidIntRadix for APIs that accept a base.",
    "Left is a returned value, not a thrown exception. Use match to choose a message or request new input. When one input contains both a syntax problem and an excessive magnitude, do not assume syntax is always reported first."
